
# Git Submodules

> For teams that prefer explicit version pinning over automatic sync.

## How to use `prompt-pack` as a Git Submodule

### Add the submodule to a target repo

```bash
# From the root of the target repo
git submodule add https://github.com/franciscosuca/prompt-pack .prompt-pack
git commit -m "chore: add prompt-pack as submodule"
```

This creates a `.prompt-pack/` directory with a pinned commit reference.

### Copy (or symlink) the files you need

Because GitHub Copilot reads `.github/copilot-instructions.md` directly, you still need to place files in the right location. After adding the submodule, copy what you need:

```bash
cp .prompt-pack/.github/copilot-instructions.md .github/copilot-instructions.md
cp .prompt-pack/.github/agents/react.agent.md   .github/agents/react.agent.md
```

Or use a symlink (works locally, but GitHub Actions won't follow it):

```bash
ln -s ../.prompt-pack/.github/copilot-instructions.md .github/copilot-instructions.md
```

### Pull upstream changes

When `prompt-pack` is updated and you want to adopt the new version:

```bash
git submodule update --remote .prompt-pack
git add .prompt-pack
git commit -m "chore: update prompt-pack to latest"
```

### Clone a repo that already has the submodule

```bash
git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/yourorg/yourrepo
# or, after a plain clone:
git submodule update --init
```
